tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1361:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1362:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1363:#  *** A PYTHON DICT LITERAL ACCEPTS A DUPLICATE KEY IN SILENCE, AND THE LAST ONE WINS.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1364:#  MEASURED ON THIS FILE, HOURS AFTER IT WAS WRITTEN. ***
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1365:#
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1366:#  Two rounds added `sgi_eaddr_probe.py` to MANIFEST independently -- the author of this
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1367:#  checker, and the #446 round whose probe it was.  Both landed, four lines apart, and
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1368:#  `len(MANIFEST)` reported ONE entry.  Nothing complained, because nothing can: by the
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1369:#  time the module object exists the earlier entry is already gone.  Here the two agreed
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1370:#  on CLASS, so the behaviour was identical and the only damage was a stale line.  Had
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1371:#  they disagreed -- one DETECTOR, one TOOL -- the file would have carried a visible,
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1372:#  reviewed, *tracked* classification that the checker silently did not use, and the
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1373:#  filename tooth that refuses `*_probe.py` in class `tool` would have been reading the
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1374:#  wrong entry while looking correct in the diff.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1375:#
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1376:#  It has to be caught by re-reading THIS FILE'S SOURCE TEXT, because the data structure
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1377:#  cannot represent the fault it suffers from.  A duplicate is a SETUP FAULT, not a
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1378:#  finding: it exits before any wiring is derived, so it can never be confused with a
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1379:#  probe that is genuinely unwired.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1380:#  The open() is INLINE rather than a call to read() below: this guard runs at import time,
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1381:#  fifty lines before read() is defined, and the first draft died with a NameError proving it.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1382:#  It failed loudly, which is luck -- a guard placed in a branch would have failed silently.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1383:def _refuse_duplicate_keys():
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1384:    with open(os.path.abspath(__file__), "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1385:        src = fh.read()
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1386:    #  *** AN ANCHOR THAT NO LONGER MATCHES IS ITSELF A SETUP FAULT, NOT A REASON TO SKIP,
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1387:    #  AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS GUARD DID `continue`. ***  A flagship review MEASURED
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1388:    #  the consequence: `MANIFEST =  {` -- one extra space, valid Python, invisible to a
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1389:    #  reader -- makes a literal find() miss, the guard skips, and a planted duplicate key
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1390:    #  runs GREEN.  That is this file's own thesis turned on itself: a fault in a guard does
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1391:    #  not fail loudly, it stops guarding.  The regex tolerates whitespace, and a miss is
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1392:    #  now reported rather than shrugged at.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1393:    for name in ("MANIFEST", "EXEMPT"):
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1394:        m = re.search(r"^%s\s*=\s*\{" % re.escape(name), src, re.M)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1395:        if m is None:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1396:            print("PROBEWIRING_SETUP_FAULT  the %s literal could not be located in this"
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1397:                  " file's own source." % name)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1398:            print("  The duplicate-key guard cannot run, and a duplicate would pass"
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1399:                  " UNSEEN.  Fix the anchor, do not delete the check.")
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1400:            sys.exit(2)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1401:        i = m.start()
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1402:        j = src.find("\n}", i)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1403:        seen, dupes = set(), []
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1404:        for k in re.findall(r'^\s*"([^"]+)"\s*:', src[i:j], re.M):
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1405:            (dupes.append(k) if k in seen else None)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1406:            seen.add(k)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1407:        if dupes:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1408:            print("PROBEWIRING_SETUP_FAULT  %s names the same key twice; a dict literal" % name)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1409:            print("  keeps only the LAST and says nothing.  Delete the stale line(s):")
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1410:            for k in sorted(set(dupes)):
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1411:                print("    %s" % k)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1412:            sys.exit(2)
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1413:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1414:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1415:_refuse_duplicate_keys()
