tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1719:def _pick_exempt_detector():
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1720:    """The selftest's exemplar is DERIVED, never hardcoded.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1721:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1722:    *** THIS FUNCTION EXISTS BECAUSE THE SELFTEST BROKE THE DAY AN EXEMPTION WAS
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1723:    DISCHARGED, WHICH IS THE ONE EVENT IT SHOULD HAVE BEEN CELEBRATING. ***  Four of its
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1724:    mutants named `pit8253_latch_probe.py` literally.  When that probe was wired into
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1725:    gate_arm.sh and its exemption deleted -- exactly the outcome the EXEMPT table exists
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1726:    to drive toward -- three mutants raised KeyError on the missing entry and the fourth
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1727:    had its premise inverted.  `precommit_check.sh` reported THE PROBE-WIRING CHECKER CAN
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1728:    NO LONGER BE SHOWN TO FAIL, correctly.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1729:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1730:    So the harness punished progress: doing the right thing to the tree broke the tool
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1731:    that asks for it.  A hardcoded exemplar is a frozen claim about a table designed to
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1732:    empty, and swapping in another name would only move the tripwire to the next
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1733:    discharge.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1734:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1735:    Any exempt DETECTOR serves: exempt means unwired, which is the precondition all four
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1736:    mutants need.  Sorted for determinism, so a failure names the same probe twice.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1737:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1738:    *** AND THE PICK IS VERIFIED, NOT MERELY CHOSEN -- BUT NOT FOR THE REASON THIS
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1739:    PARAGRAPH GAVE UNTIL 2026-08-30. ***  It said `_exline()` "reconstructs a ONE-LINE
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1740:    entry from the table", so entries wrapped across two source lines matched nothing.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1741:    `_exline()` EXTRACTS the real source text now, however many lines it spans, and its
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1742:    own docstring says so -- so the two halves of one file contradicted each other, the
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1743:    stale half sitting in the function that consumes the fresh one.  Found by the pass-2
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1744:    seat reviewing the commit that made the change.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1745:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1746:    **The `count == 1` guard is still load-bearing and its warrant has MOVED.**  It no
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1747:    longer defends against a formatting mismatch -- extraction cannot have one.  It
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1748:    defends the SELF-MATCH case: a doc comment in this file quoting an EXEMPT line makes
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1749:    the extracted text appear twice.  **The MECHANISM stated here was wrong until the
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1750:    adjudicating seat built the case: `_st_apply` does NOT silently edit the comment
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1751:    instead of the table -- it COUNTS and RAISES**, `MUTANT DID NOT APPLY: '...' x2
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1752:    (want 1)`.  So the guard prevents a selftest CRASH, not a silent wrong-edit, and
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1753:    `_st_apply`'s own raise is a second line of defence behind it.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1754:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1755:    Two facts the seat measured that belong here: injecting a verbatim copy of one
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1756:    EXEMPT entry makes `src.count()` return 2, the guard skips that name and returns
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1757:    the next candidate -- so it is genuinely load-bearing.  And **it never fires
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1758:    today**: all six counts are 1.  It is a LATENT defence, which is worth saying
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1759:    plainly, because a guard that has never fired is the one most likely to be
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1760:    deleted as dead by someone who does not know what it is for.
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1761:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1762:    *A guard whose stated reason expires but whose behaviour is still needed is the
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1763:    hardest kind to audit: deleting it looks safe, because the case it cites is gone.*
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1764:    """
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1765:    src = src_of_self()
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1766:    for name in sorted(EXEMPT):
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1767:        cls = MANIFEST.get(name, (None,))[0]
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1768:        if cls != CLASS_DETECTOR:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1769:            continue
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1770:        if src.count(_exline(name)) == 1:
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1771:            return name
tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1772:    return None
