1:#!/usr/bin/env python3
2:"""fbpending DRAIN PROBE -- a RUNG-3 detector that MEASURES the pending-interrupt
3:backlog of footbridge timer 1 in ~10 s, instead of waiting 43 s for it to overflow.
4:
5:WHY THIS EXISTS.  The rung-3 witness (fbpending_witness.py) proves the defect by
6:running until the counter wraps, which takes 42.95 s at cats' emulated_hz and cannot
7:be made faster than 31.51 s on ANY machine (the delivery ceiling is
8:TIMER_MAX_CATCHUP x TIMER_BASE_FREQUENCY = 68,157,440 increments per wall second, and
9:timer_tick0 is called from nowhere else).  A gate row cannot spend that.
10:
11:THE TRICK: MEASURE THE COUNTER, DO NOT WAIT FOR IT TO OVERFLOW.
12:
13:  1. The guest arms timer 1 at a KNOWN SLOW rate.  cats' emulated_hz is 50,000,000 and
14:     reload_timer_value() asks for emulated_hz / effective_cycles(load, ctrl, 1).  With
15:     TIMER_FCLK_256 and load 256 that is 50e6 / (256*256) = 762.94 Hz.
16:  2. It free-runs for T seconds.  Nothing acks, so the backlog is rate*T -- UNLESS the
17:     device bounds it.
18:  3. It then FREEZES the timer by writing TIMER_1_LOAD = 0x00FFFFFF while keeping
19:     TIMER_FCLK_256: the rate falls to 50e6/(0xFFFFFF*256) = 0.0116 Hz, one tick per
20:     86 s.  *** A LOAD WRITE DOES NOT CLEAR pending_timer_interrupts *** (only a
21:     TIMER_x_CONTROL write WITHOUT TIMER_ENABLE does, dev_footbridge.c:583), so the
22:     backlog survives the freeze.  That turns a moving quantity into a static one.
23:  4. It then DRAINS AND COUNTS.  DEVICE_TICK writes timer_value = random() %
24:     effective_cycles(load, ctrl, 0) whenever the backlog is > 0, and with load
25:     0x00FFFFFF that modulus is 0xFFFFFF, so the produced value is in [0, 0xFFFFFE].
26:     The guest writes the sentinel 0x00FFFFFF -- which the modulo CANNOT produce and
27:     which survives TIMER_x_VALUE's own `idata & TIMER_MAX_VAL` mask -- and spins.  A
28:     changed read means one tick was delivered; the guest then writes TIMER_1_CLEAR
29:     (one decrement) and repeats.  When the spin budget expires with the sentinel
30:     intact, the backlog is 0 and the number of decrements IS the backlog.
31:
32:  The measured number is the whole verdict: pre-fix it is rate*T.  No wrap, no 43 s,
33:  and the row reports a NUMBER rather than a boolean.
34:
35:  *** POST-FIX IT IS **NOT** THE CAP, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS PARAGRAPH SAID IT
36:  WAS. ***  Measured by a pass-2 seat on ONE binary with ONE bound of 762: backlog 3 at
37:  4 s, 100 at 46 s, 375 at 0.5 s.  Once the bound is first reached the number is a
38:  MODULAR RESIDUE of the delivered tick count, not the bound itself.
39:
40:  That is why `--expect-cap` is a one-sided `bD <= CAP` and CANNOT distinguish a bound
41:  of 762 from one of 7: the quantity it tests is structurally <= the bound whatever the
42:  bound is.  The rows that actually pin the bound's VALUE live in
43:  regress/diff_fbpending_bound.c (R5, an equality against the timer's own rate, and R6,
44:  which re-arms at a different rate) -- and they exist because a one-token mutant
45:  (`freq / 100.0`) destroyed 99% of the guest's clock while passing this probe.
46:
47:ARM X -- THE PLACEMENT DISCRIMINATOR, and the reason it is not merely a second run.
48:  dev_footbridge.c never calls timer_remove() and never lowers the core frequency when
49:  the guest DISABLES a timer: TIMER_x_CONTROL without TIMER_ENABLE zeroes the counter
50:  once (:583) and leaves the struct timer running.  So timer_tickN keeps incrementing a
51:  counter that DEVICE_TICK will never look at.  Arm X arms the timer, immediately
52:  disables it, free-runs, re-enables (which does NOT clear the counter) and then
53:  measures.  A non-zero result proves the counter accrues while the device is disabled
54:  -- which is exactly the state in which a bound placed in DEVICE_TICK cannot run.
55:
56:ARMS
57:  D  enabled throughout.  Backlog after T seconds.
58:  X  disabled during the accrual.  Backlog after T seconds.  Q1's discriminator.
59:  T  negative control: MMU left on, so the footbridge is not mapped.  Must not return
60:     the device's answers.  (machine_cats.c's boot page tables map only the low 256 MB;
61:     the footbridge is at PA 0x42000000.)
62:  K  failability control: arm D's prologue plus ONE instruction, a read of
63:     IRQ_ENABLE_SET, which dev_footbridge.c answers with fatal() then exit(1).  A probe
64:     in which nothing can die proves nothing by surviving.
65:
66:Every RAM output word is poisoned with 0xDEADBEEF first and every verdict row requires
67:the values it consults to be PRESENT.  Every planted instruction is checked, as it
68:EXECUTES, against the emulator's own disassembly (the hand-assembled-encoding trap).
