1020:#  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1021:#  #439/#440 PIT 8253 latch + LSB/MSB selector -- pitlatch / pitclobber.
1022:#
1023:#  WIRED HERE, ALONGSIDE footbridge_sites_probe.py, BECAUSE gate_hygiene.sh SAID SO.  Its
1024:#  own 17 -> 18 bump note reads: "Wiring it into gate_arm.sh alongside
1025:#  footbridge_sites_probe.py is the follow-up, and it wants an operator who can run that
1026:#  gate."  The probe execs `-E cats` and hand-assembles ARM, so this is the only gate whose
1027:#  machine it shares.
1028:#
1029:#  *** IT SHIPPED UNWIRED AND THE CARRIER SAID WHY THAT IS DANGEROUS: an unwired probe is
1030:#  exactly what shipped latent in cf4b083. ***  It has been dated-exempt in
1031:#  check_probe_wiring.py until 2026-09-20 (`wiringcliff`), and this discharges that debt --
1032:#  the exemption line comes out in the same edit, which is what stops a stale exemption
1033:#  outliving its cause.
1034:#
1035:#  CONTROLS FIRST AND SEPARATELY.  The probe's own header says it: "CONTROLS (if any is RED
1036:#  every row below is meaningless)".  C1 is a RAM liveness through the same decode, C2 reads
1037:#  CP15 c1 back to prove the MMU really went off, A1 is a device signature -- and arm T is
1038:#  the negative control that runs the same program with the MMU-disable instructions
1039:#  replaced by nops and requires it CANNOT produce the device's answer.  Under the boot-time
1040:#  MMU the low 256 MB alone is mapped, so a device row reads 0x0 WITH A LIVENESS ROW STILL
1041:#  GREEN, which is indistinguishable from an absent device.  That is why C2 and T exist and
1042:#  why a liveness row cannot stand in for them.
1043:#  ---------------------------------------------------------------- #497 widthkillclass
1044:#  *** THIS PROBE IS FOR AN SGI/MIPS MACHINE AND IT IS WIRED INTO THE GATE NAMED `arm`.
1045:#  That is following a precedent, not endorsing it. ***  This gate already runs
1046:#  footbridge_sites_probe.py and pit8253_latch_probe.py -- neither of which is an ARM
1047:#  instruction test either -- so it has become the tree's general DEVICE-probe gate by
1048:#  accretion.  *** BUT THOSE TWO AT LEAST RUN THIS GATE'S OWN `-E cats` MACHINE, AND
1049:#  THIS ONE DOES NOT: it is the FIRST probe here on a foreign machine AND CPU family.
1050:#  The PIT wiring comment says outright that it was placed here BECAUSE this is the only
1051:#  gate whose machine it shares. ***  So this is not a third instance of an existing
1052:#  accretion; it is a new kind of one, and the sentence above is true while its
1053:#  implication was generous to the decision it defends.  The architecture-named alternative, gate_mips.sh, is an 82-line RIG-BOOT
1054:#  gate with no probe infrastructure at all; putting a 35-row detector there would change
1055:#  what that gate is for.
1056:#
1057:#  Recorded rather than silently compounded: the right answer is a gate_devices.sh split,
1058:#  which is a harness round of its own and owes its own controls.  FILED as
1059:#  `gatedevicesplit`, with a forcing criterion: the NEXT device probe wired into
1060:#  this gate converts it from filed to OWED.
1:#!/bin/bash
2:# GATE 10 -- SuperH floating-point rounding mode, measured on real guest instructions.
3:#
4:# What this protects
5:# -----------------
6:# #296 wired FPSCR.RM into thirteen of the sixteen single-precision store sites in
7:# cpu_sh_instr.c. Before it, the field was guest-writable and decoded NOWHERE: every store
8:# truncated. Truncation is round-toward-zero, which is the mode the SH-4 resets into, so
9:# there was no visible defect until you look at a guest that CHANGES the mode -- and
10:# OpenBSD/landisk installs round-to-nearest at every exec.
11:#
12:# Why the vectors look the way they do
13:# -----------------------------------
14:# Each one runs under BOTH modes and expects DIFFERENT answers, so reverting #296 turns the
15:# gate red. Two vectors are deliberately mode-INDEPENDENT and named PIN: they record limits
16:# that are real and are NOT fixed (double precision cannot be fixed at the store, and the
17:# host-double double-rounding is shared by every CPU core). A pin failing means someone
18:# changed a known limitation without updating the record -- which is worth knowing either
19:# way, and is the opposite of a check that cannot fail.
20:#
21:# The gate asserts on the number of discriminating vectors as well as on the results,
22:# because a table where every vector wants the same answer in both modes would pass
23:# vacuously. That is the failure mode this harness has now been bitten by five times.
24:set -u
25:cd "$(dirname "$0")"
26:. ./lib.sh
27:
28:gate_begin "gate 10: SuperH rounding mode (#296)"
29:
30:PMAX=${PMAX:-$ROOT/build/gxemul}
31:KERNEL=$IMAGES/openbsd76-landisk-bsd.rd
32:LOG=$LOGDIR/gate_sh_rounding.log
33:
34:need_exec "$PMAX"
35:need_file "$KERNEL"
36:command -v python3 >/dev/null || gate_skip "python3 not available"
37:
38:note "binary : $PMAX"
39:note "kernel : $KERNEL (loaded so the machine constructs; never executed)"
40:note "cold debugger, no media, no console -- the SCIF drops host->guest writes (#293)"
41:
42:python3 sh_rounding_probe.py "$PMAX" "$KERNEL" > "$LOG" 2>&1 || true
43:
44:if ! grep -q "SH_ROUND_RESULT=" "$LOG"; then
45:    note "probe produced no result line; last lines follow"
46:    tail -5 "$LOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
47:    gate_skip "probe did not complete"
48:fi
49:
946:#  ------------------------------------------------------------------ wdcflood
947:#  Six unlatched fatal() in dev_wdc.c, each guest-drivable without bound.  Measured
948:#  pre-fix: 20 stores -> 20 host lines with AND without -q, and the OVER-READ site at
949:#  500 iterations produced 2000 lines / 158,449 bytes.  Artifacts live at
950:#  regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/{fr_no__q,fr_with__q,n3}.out.
951:#  (An earlier wording said "the read path", which points at the non-8-bit READ guard;
952:#  n3.out is 2000x "reading too much", i.e. the ring over-read.  Same numbers, wrong
953:#  site named.)  fatal() has no early-out (core/debugmsg.c:384-390 against debug()'s
954:#  at :375), so -q cannot silence it.
955:#
956:#  THE DENOMINATOR IS 26 AND TWELVE OF THOSE ROWS EXIST BECAUSE A PANEL OR A KILL TABLE
957:#  FOUND THE FIRST FOURTEEN INSUFFICIENT.  Every one of the twelve closes a MEASURED or
958:#  PREDICTED escape, and each is the only row that sees its own:
959:#    overrun x2  the :145 reinstatement -- an L2 FLOOR BREACH until this row existed
960:#    interleave  a latch reset on a NORMAL operation, invisible to back-to-back rows
961:#    w16 reset   a latch operand a guest can CLEAR between fires
962:#    r16 reset   the same at the READ site -- sibling parity, and it was missing
963:#    fire3       a period-N latch; a two-fire oracle cannot see period 2
964:#    w32/r32     the width rows probed 16-bit only
965:#    r16/w16 value  BOTH fires, because a `return` after a latched fatal() corrupts
966:#                   the FIRST access and an earlier one-value form was VACUOUS
967:#    wr indep    TWO SITES IN ONE SESSION -- the only row that can see flag pairing
968:#    w mixed     a width-KEYED latch: alternating mov.w/mov.l floods forever, and no
969:#    r mixed     single-width row can see it.  Both came from the capped ask-again,
970:#                fired against a table that was already clean at 24 rows.
971:#
972:#  -u IS LOAD-BEARING, for the reason the gdrom section above records: with stdout
973:#  redirected to a file it is block buffered, and a killed run then loses every row and
974:#  looks like a run that produced nothing.
975:#
976:#  THE DISK IS REQUIRED AND ITS ABSENCE IS A SKIP, NEVER A PASS.  wdc_command()
977:#  early-returns before the opcode switch when no IDE image exists (grep
978:#  `but no disk image`), so without it the :583 rows cannot fire at all and would read
979:#  as green-on-nothing.  An earlier form cited :461-468, which is wdc_status() and was
980:#  wrong even before the fix moved every line in this file.
981:#
982:#  THAT EARLY RETURN'S OWN debug() REPEATS ONCE PER FAILED COMMAND and is NOT latched
983:#  here.  It is a DIFFERENT CHANNEL, and the reason is stronger than -q: debug()
984:#  computes v = verbose - (emul_executing ? 1 : 0) + (stepping ? 1 : 0) and returns at
985:#  core/debugmsg.c:375 when v < 0.  FREE-RUNNING AT DEFAULT VERBOSITY THAT IS v == -1,
1380:    fi
1381:fi
1382:
1383:WDCIMG=$IMAGES/wdc-scratch-64m.img
1384:if [ ! -f "$WDCIMG" ]; then
1385:    gate_skip "wdcflood: $WDCIMG absent (see regress/images.md -- it is a dd recipe, not a download)"
1386:else
1387:    WFLOG=$LOGDIR/gate_wdc_flood.log
1388:    python3 -u wdc_flood_probe.py "$PMAX" "$KERNEL" "$WDCIMG" > "$WFLOG" 2>&1 || true
1389:
1390:    if ! grep -q "WDC_FLOOD_RESULT=" "$WFLOG"; then
1391:        note "wdc_flood probe produced no result line; last lines follow"
1392:        tail -5 "$WFLOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
1393:        check "wdc_flood probe completed" "no" "yes"
1394:    else
1395:        grep -E " ok$| FAIL$" "$WFLOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
1396:        wfres=$(grep -o "WDC_FLOOD_RESULT=[0-9]*/[0-9]*" "$WFLOG" | tail -1 | cut -d= -f2)
1397:
1398:        #  *** PARSE BEFORE SCORE. ***  If the result line exists but is MALFORMED, the
1399:        #  outer grep still succeeds, `wfres` is EMPTY, and both `${wfres%/*}` and
1400:        #  `${wfres#*/}` expand to the empty string -- so `check "rows correct" "" ""`
1401:        #  PASSES while nothing was measured.  Three panel seats found that false pass
1402:        #  in the asic section above; it is the project's own "a pattern that matched
1403:        #  nothing reports zero for everything" class in shell-expansion clothes.
1404:        if [ -z "$wfres" ]; then
1405:            note "wdc_flood result line present but unparseable; last lines follow"
1406:            tail -3 "$WFLOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
1407:            check "wdc_flood result parses" "no" "yes"
1408:        else
1409:            check "wdc_flood result parses" "yes" "yes"
1410:            check "wdc_flood rows run"     "${wfres#*/}" 26
1411:            check "wdc_flood rows correct" "${wfres%/*}" "${wfres#*/}"
1412:
1413:            #  Per-row presence.  A row that silently stops being tested is the
1414:            #  padded-column class.  The probe's format is "%-14s " -- fourteen columns
1415:            #  plus one literal space -- so a name of length L is followed by (15 - L)
1416:            #  spaces; at L=13 that is two and the `^$v  ` anchor still matches, at L=14
1417:            #  it is ONE and THE ROW SILENTLY STOPS BEING TESTED while the gate stays
1418:            #  green.  Longest name here is `overread x2` / `unimpl 2op` (11), so there

