VERDICT: The six exemptions are confirmed, with rejection starting on 2026-09-21. Their setup costs differ substantially, and several completion markers do not establish success.

The checker’s `EXEMPT` dictionary contains exactly the six probes assessed below, all dated 2026-09-20 and all classified as detectors. Its comparison is `d < today`, so the exemption remains valid on September 20. A detected gate invocation with an exemption still present produces a separate `STALE EXEMPTION` failure. [Checker exemptions and expiry logic](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:1326).

`gate_wdc.sh` does not exist. I count **16 gates** in `run.sh`, in this order: `gate_build`, `gate_offline`, `selftest_mutation`, `gate_mips`, `gate_crossfamily`, `gate_hygiene`, `gate_ab`, `gate_upstream`, `gate_asan_sweep`, `gate_sh_rounding`, `gate_m88k_rounding`, `gate_mips_rounding`, `gate_ppc`, `gate_arm`, `gate_ppc_halt`, and `gate_mips_folds`. None contains an invocation of these six probes; hygiene’s references inspect source text. [Gate array](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/run.sh:10).

There is a limit to that verification: the checker also scans `run.sh` and `lib.sh`, derives its Python-file population recursively, and reads other probes’ summaries. Running it would exceed your allowlist. I therefore confirmed **six expiring exemption entries**, without independently reproducing “exactly six total problems” or verifying the commit-hook connection. The checker recognizes static interpreter call sites; it does not execute the probes itself.

The timings below are source-based estimates, not measured runs.

1. **[fbpending_drain_probe.py](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/fbpending_drain_probe.py:75)** takes an emulator binary and an existing four-byte raw loader stub. Its options are `--accrue` (default 4 seconds), `--expect-cap` (default −1), `--timer` (default index 0), `--learn`, `--fast`, and `--only`. It constructs the in-tree CATS machine through a Unix PTY; it needs no disk image or booted operating system.

   The essential prerequisite is a justified `--expect-cap` value. The default is report-only. With a cap, success requires both measured backlogs to be bounded, the controls to pass, and neither clock to be dead. This measures an upper bound; it does **not** establish that the bound preserves the intended clock rate. `--only` also leaves required controls or measurements absent, so it does not provide the normal full verdict.

   The header advertises approximately 10 seconds, but the full default execution has **five arms: K, E, T, D, and X**. Accrual alone totals 9 seconds; draining and instrumentation add time. Think tens of seconds on a healthy run. D and X each allow 30 seconds for draining, T allows 4, and prompt waits are additional inactivity deadlines rather than an overall timeout.

   Its natural home is **`gate_arm.sh`, gate 14**: that gate already creates the loader stub and runs Footbridge probes on CATS. [Existing setup and Footbridge coverage](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/gate_arm.sh:966).

   There is **no separate DONE sentinel**. The terminal result is `FBDRAIN_VERDICT=PASS`, `FBDRAIN_VERDICT=FAIL`, or `FBDRAIN_VERDICT=REPORT-ONLY`. Supporting lines include `FBDRAIN_CONTROL=OK` or `FBDRAIN_CONTROL=FAILED`, numeric `FBDRAIN_BACKLOG_D=` and `FBDRAIN_BACKLOG_X=`, and `FBDRAIN_BOUNDED=YES` or `FBDRAIN_BOUNDED=NO` with a cap suffix. Failed controls can lead to `REPORT-ONLY` even when a cap was supplied. `PROBE_WALL=` is absent from the PASS/FAIL branch because that branch exits first.

2. **[hpcmips_ctor_probe.py](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/hpcmips_ctor_probe.py:371)** accepts `--binary`, `--source`, and `--timeout`. The binary defaults to `$GXROOT/build/gxemul`, with a repository-layout fallback when `GXROOT` is unset. Source selection first checks beside the binary, then falls back to the committed `machine_hpcmips.c`.

   It needs an executable emulator, GNU `stdbuf`, matching machine source, and writable temporary storage. It creates its own raw loader fixture and two-machine configuration. No disk image or OS rig is required. It enumerates subtypes from the binary, exercises construction with ordinary and slave-console flags, and performs source checks; the file explicitly says it does not exercise X11.

   The exemption estimates approximately 8 seconds. The default timeout is **40 seconds per subprocess**, across subtype runs and controls, so it is not a 40-second overall limit. Additional behavioral dependencies matter: a control expects an unrelated `mvmeppc/mvme1600` abort, and the residual expectation pins three UART-related cases—`be-300`, `e-105`, and `agenda`—as needing slave consoles.

   Its natural home is **`gate_asan_sweep.sh`, gate 9**, whose purpose is machine and subtype construction. That gate already handles `stdbuf` with ASan’s required runtime settings. Its ASan binary remains an external prerequisite, and the probe’s source must correspond to the selected binary. [Construction gate](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/gate_asan_sweep.sh:92).

   There is **no DONE token**. It prints `HPCMIPS_CTOR_RESULT=<passed>/<total>`, followed by `HPCMIPS_CTOR_PASS` or `HPCMIPS_CTOR_FAIL`, with a corresponding process status. The complete expected result is `HPCMIPS_CTOR_RESULT=28/28`. Those 28 rows comprise P1–P3, F1–F5, E1, A1–A5, B1, C1–C5, S0–S5, R1, and I1.

3. **[wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py:189)** takes positional base binary, fixed binary, CD image, and output directory arguments, with historical `/tmp/gxadd/` defaults. `FIXBIN` supplies the fixed-binary fallback; `FIXTAG` changes its label.

   This really does require **two emulator runs** and specially prepared media. It constructs unmodified Malta and attaches the image as a CD-ROM using `ciR:`. The oracle depends on known disk-content anchors across a two-chunk transfer; an arbitrary CD image is insufficient. The script generates its guest program and transcripts, but **does not generate the required media**.

   Fixed settling and register-reading waits contribute approximately 31 seconds across both legs, before additional guest execution time. Each leg has a **300-second breakpoint-wait budget**, plus surrounding overhead. This is a materially larger obligation than an image-free constructor check.

   Its nearest existing home is **`gate_sh_rounding.sh`, gate 10**, through that gate’s WDC coverage. However, the existing `wdc-scratch-64m.img` prerequisite does not establish the prepared-content requirement. [Existing WDC lane](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/gate_sh_rounding.sh:1383).

   Completion is exactly `ADDTOINBUF_RUNG3_DONE`. The stable count line, preceded by two spaces, is `<N> of 30 predictions correct across both legs`; complete agreement produces `30 of 30 predictions correct across both legs`. There is **no aggregate PASS/FAIL verdict**, and that count does not incorporate breakpoint or encoding validity. Ordinary row failures still finish with process status zero.

   Its table contains **15 rows**: seven controls—RAM, two FIFO reads, status, two disk anchors, and the drain counter—four defect rows covering three returned bytes and completion status, and four acceptance rows covering two bytes after each of two command types.

4. **[wdc_altoffset_probe.py](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/wdc_altoffset_probe.py:133)** takes base binary, fixed binary, disk image, and optional output directory, with historical `/tmp/gxaltoff/` defaults. Its public execution path always runs both binaries.

   It constructs Malta, attaches ordinary IDE media using `iR:`, and generates its own raw guest program. It needs a writable output directory and Unix PTY support, but no OS image or external rig. Unlike addtoinbuf, its oracle does not depend on specially prepared disk contents. The historical baseline must satisfy its explicit pre-fix expectations; merely supplying two builds is insufficient.

   Expect approximately **34 seconds for both legs** when the guest reaches its breakpoint promptly. Each leg permits another 120-second breakpoint wait, with overhead outside that budget.

   Its nearest device home is **`gate_sh_rounding.sh`, gate 10**, alongside the existing WDC section and scratch-media prerequisite. The shared coverage is the controller; these probes use Malta, whereas that gate’s primary machine is Landisk.

   Completion is `ALTOFF_DET_DONE`. The exact positive verdict is `VERDICT   WITNESS CONFIRMED -- the committed build treats 0x3f7 as 0x3f6 and the fix leg separates them`, with three spaces after `VERDICT`. Failure prints `VERDICT   *** SOMETHING DID NOT HOLD -- read the FAIL lines above ***`. Its count line reaches `36 of 36 predictions correct across both legs`.

   Unlike addtoinbuf’s count, this verdict includes row agreement, both breakpoint hits, and encoding checks. It is a stable textual verdict, **but a failed verdict still returns process status zero**.

5. **[wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py:51)** accepts output directory, disk image, and subsequent `tag=binary` arguments. Contrary to the exemption’s blanket description, its parser does **not require two legs**: one is accepted, and even zero legs reach the completion marker.

   It imports the adjacent `wdc_altoffset_probe.py` for its encoder and PTY machinery, then replaces the guest program. It needs ordinary IDE media, a writable output directory, and a Malta-capable binary. No specially prepared image contents or OS boot are required. Budget approximately **14 seconds per binary** when healthy; its breakpoint wait allows 90 seconds per leg.

   Its nearest home is **`gate_sh_rounding.sh`, gate 10**, for the same WDC device coverage and ordinary scratch-media requirement as the main probe.

   Completion is `ALTOFF_4BIT_DONE`. There is **no aggregate success result**. The count text is `<N> of <M> PREDICTED cells correct (the P5 pair is recorded, not predicted --`; the denominator is seven per leg and excludes the critical defect pair. Ignoring indentation, its positive per-leg text is `0x3f7 0x0e -> declined (the fix's behaviour)`. Alternatives report `*** RESET PERFORMED -- DEFECT PRESENT ***` or an `INDETERMINATE` result.

   Those measurements, control failures, encoding counts, and breakpoint readiness do not determine a failing process status.

6. **[wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py:61)** likewise accepts output directory, image, and `tag=binary` arguments, with no enforced minimum leg count. It imports the main probe and supplies a different guest program.

   Its distinguishing prerequisite is **CD-ROM attachment semantics**, supplied internally through `ciR:`. It measures the ATAPI signature, not disk payload bytes. Therefore the file does not establish a requirement for a bootable ISO or poisoned media; existing scratch-image bytes are a plausible media source, subject to successful attachment and its channel controls. This is substantially different from addtoinbuf’s content oracle.

   Budget approximately **14 seconds per binary** when healthy, with a 60-second breakpoint-wait budget per leg. Its nearest existing device home is **`gate_sh_rounding.sh`, gate 10**, although that gate’s existing ordinary-disk lane does not itself prove this CD-ROM channel works.

   Completion is `ALTOFF_SIG_DONE`. There is **no aggregate result count or enforced PASS/FAIL**. Ignoring indentation, the per-leg line is `a store to the READ-ONLY 0x3f7 latched the ATAPI signature: no -- declined`, or the same prefix ending `YES -- DEFECT PRESENT`.

   The positive wording is unsafe as a standalone success test: any values other than the defect signature—including missing values—take the `no -- declined` branch. Liveness, CD-ROM recognition, breakpoint readiness, and encoding observations are reported separately without enforcing failure.

For **Q3**, the three altoffset probes share infrastructure and some controls, but cover different properties:

- **Main: 18 rows, 36 predictions across its two legs.** Six initial rows cover RAM, two cylinder-register readbacks, two FIFO reads, and computed status. The remaining twelve form four groups of three: armed-state liveness plus status and queued-data observations for a legal reset, an invalid-offset reset, an invalid-offset interrupt-mask-only case, and an invalid-offset bare-reset case. **Four rows differ between baseline and fixed expectations**, including the later P4 pair; comments referring to only two are stale. [Row table](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/wdc_altoffset_probe.py:355).
- **Signature: eight observations.** These are RAM, two initial cylinder readbacks, two post-invalid-offset cylinder readings, one intermediate cylinder reading, and two legal-reset signature readings. Four have explicit expected values; the other four are recorded. It exposes reset-command state changes that leave DRQ and queued data intact. It does not enforce those observations as assertions.
- **4-bit: nine observations.** These are RAM, two cylinder readbacks, two armed-state checks, two invalid-offset outcomes, and two legal-reset outcomes. Seven are predicted controls; the critical two are recorded. It covers the control-value case with the 4-bit flag set that the other probes miss.

One execution of a gate can encompass all three separate probe runs. **No existing single probe invocation supplies all three measurements.** Importing the main file does not execute its main test, and the signature channel requires different media classification. All three also leave alternate-region reads uncovered.

For **Q5**, my operational classification is **two enforcing detectors**—fbpending with a meaningful cap, and hpcmips—**two mixed witness/detector differentials**—main altoffset and addtoinbuf—and **two report-only measurement instruments**—signature and 4-bit. I find **zero purely pre-fix, symptom-only witnesses among these six**.

In particular, addtoinbuf’s `WITNESS` heading is incomplete evidence: its fixed-side expectations require disk-correct data and preserved legal behavior. Main altoffset similarly carries repaired-property expectations alongside baseline symptom expectations. The two reporting companions observe repaired behavior but lack enforcement. The manifest’s detector classification expresses that intended durable coverage; it does not make their current execution contracts gate-ready.
